inside the Prime Minister was giving a  speech about preparedness for future  bush fires outside protesters were  beyond angry a month in toxic smoke it's  not anger it's just it's fury it's  something that I haven't felt before we  have a government that is not only in  denial they are promoting fossil fuels  they are promoting coal protesters and  environmentalists say the ferocity of  Australia's fires are evidence of  climate change what frustrates many  Australians is Australian politicians  including the Prime Minister play down  the link between climate change and the  recent bush fires while pursuing  policies that could make climate change  worse Australia exports more coal than  any other country it is the world's  third largest exporter of all fossil  fuel products measured by their  potential to emit carbon dioxide behind  Saudi Arabia and Russia and Australia  plans to grow its export significantly  the government has given approval to the  Indian company Adani to build a coal  mine that was intended to be the biggest  in Australia and one of the biggest in  the world it will initially export 10  million tonnes of coal a year but the  railway from the mine to the coast will  have the capacity to carry 14 million  tonnes a year  protestors fighting the project have  targeted adonys contractors recently  here in Munich outside the German  company designing its railway signalling  system and they've also been  demonstrating against financial backers  making it hard for a Doni to borrow for  the project the company is now financing  it itself and has had to scale back its  initial plans  critics say such projects show Australia  needs to reverse its priorities stopping  new mines would put upward pressure on  coal prices making it less attractive to  burn coal will also incentivize money to  flow into the alternative forms of  energy that we need a Donnie says  criticism of its mining operations is  unfair another company's mines have  government approval to become much  bigger Australia's Prime Minister has  towns damaged by the fires but says he's  committed to protecting mining jobs he  will also not reduce the number of  coal-fired power stations in the world  today by forcing the shutdown of  Australian coal mines and Australian  jobs that go with them so the countries  will just buy the coal from somewhere  else  critics disagree but just as the  bushfire still rage so do the arguments  of over government policy Andrew Thomas  al-jazeera Canberra
 

